# Use the official n8n image as the base.
#
# PINNED, and it must stay pinned. `:latest` was the documented rolling tier until 2026-08-19, on
# the premise that a rebuild picks up the newest base — but the thing that forced those rebuilds
# was roles/containers/common/tasks/redeploy_cron.yml, whose callers the k3s migration archived on
# 2026-08-14. Nothing on the cluster side replaced it, so a `:latest` FROM has no updater at all:
# Renovate has no version to bump, so there is no PR, no commit, and no gitops tick. n8n sat on
# 2.34.6 while upstream shipped 2.35.4.
#
# An explicit ref is what Renovate's built-in dockerfile manager reads. It raises a PR (no
# automerge — the automerge rules match custom.regex managers only), and the merged commit lands
# under ansible/roles/k8s/**, which gitops_deploy classifies as a k8s-role change and Discord-alerts
# for an operator to deploy. That is the posture n8n's autodeploy-denylist entry asks for.
#
# CHANNEL + DIGEST, not a version. `:2.35.4` was the first pin, and it had a side effect worth
# naming: it changed which release train n8n follows. `:latest` is an alias for `:stable`, so every
# rebuild had been quietly tracking the STABLE channel; pinning a bare version froze that and handed
# the update stream to Renovate's docker datasource, which reads every published tag and so runs
# AHEAD of stable — its next offer would have been 2.36.2, a release upstream has not promoted.
#
# Pinning the channel tag with a digest beside it restores the old behaviour and makes it explicit.
# The tag names the channel; the digest is what Renovate bumps, so a PR arrives exactly when n8n
# promotes a new stable, and an unpromoted release is never offered. Same shape as the four
# line-tagged bases digest-pinned in PR #266 — the tag holds the line, the digest tracks movement
# inside it.
#
# LOCKSTEP with Dockerfile-runners.j2: n8n and its task runners are version-coupled, both
# repositories publish a `stable` tag and both resolved to 2.35.4 when this was written, and
# renovate.json groups them into one PR. test_renovate_managers.py asserts the two carry the same
# TAG — their digests differ by design, since they are different images.
FROM n8nio/n8n:stable@sha256:14c4285bc3034dc5b51034aea393711d27053588e460722bce523453a626f23c

# Switch to the root user to install packages
USER root

# Force npm to use /usr/local
ENV NPM_CONFIG_PREFIX=/usr/local

# Add your npm packages here.
# Use 'RUN npm install -g ...'
# Example:
RUN npm install -g fuzzball

# Switch back to the non-privileged 'node' user
USER node
